# LOOM-SCAFFOLD-MARKER: environment-promotion
#
# rehearsal-<ENV>.conf — THE REHEARSAL ATTESTATION.
#
# WHO WRITES THIS FILE: your rehearsal/staging deploy seam, at the end of a
# rehearsal run. Not the harness. LogicLoom never writes it, never runs a
# rehearsal, and never calls a CI API to find out whether one happened.
#
# WHO READS IT: `.logic-loom/scripts/bash/promote-gate.sh`, and only when a
# promotion stage asks for the rehearsal contract (`/promote-prod` does).
#
# WHERE IT LIVES: <state-dir>/rehearsal-<ENV>.conf, where <ENV> is the
# environment named by the production block's `promotes_from`, and <state-dir>
# is $LOOM_PROMOTION_STATE_DIR, else .logic-loom/state/.
#
# GRAMMAR: `key = value`, one per line, `#` starts a comment. Parsed as text,
# never sourced, never evaluated — the same posture as environments.conf.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE HONEST SPLIT — verified vs. taken on trust
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VERIFIED BY THE HARNESS: that this file exists and is readable; that `status`
# is `success`; that `completed_at` parses and falls inside the staleness bound;
# that `rehearsed_commit` is present; and that the commit being promoted is
# either exactly `rehearsed_commit` or is listed in `covers_commits`.
#
# TAKEN ON TRUST: that a rehearsal actually ran, that it actually passed, and
# that `rehearsed_commit` really is an ANCESTOR of what is being promoted. The
# harness runs no git and calls no CI API. It cannot check ancestry. It checks
# that you DECLARED an answer, and the declaration is only as good as the seam
# that wrote it.
#
# THIS IS THE WHOLE CONTRACT. If that trust is not good enough for your blast
# radius, the fix is a stronger seam — one that computes ancestry with git and
# refuses to write this file when it cannot — not a stronger harness.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE § 6.4 TRAP — read this before writing `rehearsed_commit`
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# If your staging branch is periodically rebuilt from production and then merged
# with the integration branch, it is NEVER a pure mirror of either. The commit
# that was actually rehearsed is the MERGE'S SECOND PARENT, not the merge head.
#
# A seam that writes the merge head here writes a PLAUSIBLE WRONG ANSWER, and
# the gate will accept it — an ancestry check usually returns a believable
# result rather than an error, which is exactly what makes this trap quiet.
#
# If your seam cannot unambiguously identify the rehearsed commit, it must write
#   status = failure
# and say why. Never guess at a parent (policy § 4.2, § 6.4).
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FIELDS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The environment that was rehearsed. Must match the production block's
# `promotes_from`.
environment      = __ENV_NAME__

# success | failure. Anything that is not exactly `success` refuses the
# production promotion, with no override. Write `failure` — with a `note` — for
# any run that did not complete, including one whose result you could not
# determine. There is no third state.
status           = failure

# ISO-8601 UTC, e.g. 2026-08-22T14:03:00Z. An unparseable value REFUSES the
# promotion rather than being treated as recent (policy § 4.2).
completed_at     =

# The commit this rehearsal ACTUALLY exercised. See the § 6.4 trap above.
rehearsed_commit =

# OPTIONAL, space-separated. Commits your seam ASSERTS are descendants of
# `rehearsed_commit` — i.e. commits it is willing to have promoted on the
# strength of this rehearsal. Leave it empty and the gate accepts only an exact
# match on `rehearsed_commit`, which is the conservative default.
#
# Every sha listed here is a claim your seam is making with git in hand. Do not
# widen it by hand to get a promotion through; that is the same instinct as
# widening a seed allowlist, and it fails the same way.
covers_commits   =

# Free text carried into the refusal message on a failure. Say what broke and
# where to look. A failed rehearsal with no note is a red verdict nobody can act
# on.
note             =

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AFTER A FAILED PRODUCTION PROMOTION — do not tidy up
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Policy § 4.5: teardown of the rehearsal environment belongs in the PRODUCTION
# release pipeline, gated on every deploy step succeeding. On a production
# failure, teardown is intentionally SKIPPED. The surviving rehearsal
# environment is the diagnostic diff between "the rehearsal passed" and
# "production failed" — the single most useful artifact the failure produced.
# The instinct to clean up the mess destroys it.
